@page "/traps"

<PageTitle>Common Traps — BlazorEnvDemo</PageTitle>

<h1>⚠️ Common Traps — Demonstrated</h1>

<p>
    Each trap below is a real mistake developers make when trying to set the client environment
    for Blazor WASM without using <code>WasmApplicationEnvironmentName</code>.
    This page pairs each trap with an explanation of why it fails.
</p>

<div class="trap-list">

    <div class="trap-card trap-fail">
        <div class="trap-header">
            <span class="trap-status">❌ Trap 1</span>
            <strong>Naming a publish profile "UAT" without setting the WASM property</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
            A publish profile named <code>UAT.pubxml</code> with no <code>WasmApplicationEnvironmentName</code>
            has no effect on the client bundle environment. The name of the profile is cosmetic only.
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;!-- UAT.pubxml — THIS DOES NOTHING for the client env --&gt;
&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;
  &lt;PublishProvider&gt;FileSystem&lt;/PublishProvider&gt;
  &lt;!-- Missing: WasmApplicationEnvironmentName --&gt;
&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;</code></pre>
        <p class="fix">
            ✅ Fix: Add <code>&lt;WasmApplicationEnvironmentName&gt;UAT&lt;/WasmApplicationEnvironmentName&gt;</code>
            inside the PropertyGroup, or pass it at the command line.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap-card trap-fail">
        <div class="trap-header">
            <span class="trap-status">❌ Trap 2</span>
            <strong>Setting only ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT on Azure App Service</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
            App Service Application Settings like <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT = UAT</code> affect
            the <em>server process</em> only (configuration loading, middleware, hosted services).
            The Blazor WASM client is a static bundle — it was already compiled with an environment
            baked in. The app setting does not republish the client.
        </p>
        <div class="finding-box finding-warning">
            Server → UAT ✅ &nbsp;|&nbsp; Client bundle → still Production ❌
        </div>
        <p class="fix">
            ✅ Fix: Publish a separate artifact per environment using
            <code>-p:WasmApplicationEnvironmentName=UAT</code>. Deploy the UAT artifact to the UAT slot.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap-card trap-fail">
        <div class="trap-header">
            <span class="trap-status">❌ Trap 3</span>
            <strong>Using launchSettings.json to control publish environment</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
            <code>Properties/launchSettings.json</code> is a developer convenience file. The .NET SDK
            explicitly ignores it during <code>dotnet publish</code>. Changing
            <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code> there will not affect any deployed build.
        </p>
        <pre><code>// launchSettings.json — IGNORED during dotnet publish
{
  "profiles": {
    "UAT": {
      "environmentVariables": {
        "ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT": "UAT"   // ← has zero effect on publish output
      }
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
        <p class="fix">
            ✅ Fix: Drive the environment from the pipeline using
            <code>dotnet publish -p:WasmApplicationEnvironmentName=$(WasmEnv)</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap-card trap-fail">
        <div class="trap-header">
            <span class="trap-status">❌ Trap 4</span>
            <strong>Placing appsettings.UAT.json in the project root (not wwwroot)</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
            For standalone Blazor WASM, configuration files must be in
            <code>wwwroot/</code> so that the browser can fetch them as static assets.
            A file at the project root is not served to the browser.
        </p>
        <pre><code>BlazorApp/
├── appsettings.UAT.json     ← ❌ not served to browser
└── wwwroot/
    └── appsettings.UAT.json ← ✅ correct location</code></pre>
        <p class="fix">
            ✅ Fix: Move all client appsettings files under <code>wwwroot/</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap-card trap-fail">
        <div class="trap-header">
            <span class="trap-status">❌ Trap 5</span>
            <strong>Expecting one published output to serve all environments</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
            The client environment and appsettings selection are baked into the publish output
            (specifically <code>blazor.boot.json</code> and the loaded configuration).
            You cannot deploy one artifact to UAT and Production and have each load different settings.
        </p>
        <p class="fix">
            ✅ Fix: Publish separate artifacts per environment and deploy the matching artifact to
            the matching slot. Use pipeline stage variables to parameterise <code>WasmApplicationEnvironmentName</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="trap-card trap-fail">
        <div class="trap-header">
            <span class="trap-status">❌ Trap 6</span>
            <strong>Having appsettings.UAT.json in the repo but not publishing it</strong>
        </div>
        <p>
            If <code>appsettings.UAT.json</code> is not included in the publish output
            (due to implicit exclusion or misconfiguration), the WASM runtime will silently
            fall back to <code>appsettings.json</code> only—with no error.
        </p>
        <p class="fix">
            ✅ Fix: Add an explicit <code>&lt;None Include=...&gt;</code> item in the csproj
            with <code>CopyToPublishDirectory="PreserveNewest"</code> (already done in this project).
        </p>
    </div>

</div>
